A small fix in app/lib/widgets/lander_view.dart only. I verified it: all 5 repro cases had no exception, and test/widget/lander_view_test.dart passed 16/16. I then reverted it, so the worktree is clean.

(1) At line 528, change `final viewH` to `var viewH` and add these lines after it:
```dart
final scroll = widget.maxHeight.isFinite && viewH + 4 + 48.0 * rows + notesH > widget.maxHeight;
if (scroll) viewH = math.max(0.0, math.min(viewH, widget.maxHeight - 4 - 48));
```

(2) Replace the returned SizedBox/Column (lines 674-682) with this:
```dart
final below = [buttons, for (final n in notes) Text(n, style: PHText.small.copyWith(color: PH.muted), maxLines: 4, overflow: TextOverflow.ellipsis)];
return SizedBox(width: w, height: scroll ? widget.maxHeight : null,
  child: Column(mainAxisSize: MainAxisSize.min, crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.stretch, children: [
    ClipRRect(borderRadius: BorderRadius.circular(PH.radiusInner), child: SizedBox(height: viewH, child: view)),
    const SizedBox(height: 4),
    if (scroll) Expanded(child: SingleChildScrollView(child: Column(crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.stretch, children: below))) else ...below,
  ]));
```

This removes the overflow. The first button row, with Face the Sun, always stays on screen, and the rest of the buttons and the notes scroll under the view. The scroll is only around the buttons and notes, so it does not compete with the view's scale gesture. Portrait and wide layouts (infinite maxHeight) are unchanged.

(3) Add a regression test in test/widget/lander_view_test.dart. Pump LightTab in the 851x393 window with the 771x290 slot, and in the 891x411 window with the 735x228 slot. Tap 'Lander', call meshReady, then assert `tester.takeException()` is null and that the 'Face the Sun' tooltip rect lies inside the LanderViewPanel rect.

Optional, separate from the fix: to get closer to §7.2 (the view at full height), the short layout in light_map.dart could give the lander panel a width of c.maxWidth - 16 - 240 instead of side. The buttons then fit in one row. The scroll fallback above is still needed for the 172-dp-tall case.

The smallest correct fix keeps the results bit-identical, and the existing 'sliced build equals one-shot' test still covers that.

(1) In core/lander_view.dart, replace the one-shot `_radix` with a resumable class whose units each handle at most 65,536 elements. Per 16-bit pass the units are: histogram chunks, then one prefix-sum unit, then scatter chunks, then a buffer swap and a cnt reset. For example:

```
class _Radix {
  final Uint32List keys;
  Int32List src, dst;
  final cnt = Int32List(65537);
  int pass = 0, stage = 0, i = 0;
  _Radix(Int32List ids, this.keys) : src = ids, dst = Int32List(ids.length);
  bool get done => pass == 2;
  void unit() {
    final s = 16 * pass, n = src.length;
    if (stage == 0) {
      final e = math.min(n, i + 65536);
      for (; i < e; i++) cnt[((keys[src[i]] >> s) & 0xFFFF) + 1]++;
      if (i == n) { stage = 1; i = 0; }
    } else if (stage == 1) {
      for (var k = 1; k < 65537; k++) cnt[k] += cnt[k - 1];
      stage = 2;
    } else {
      final e = math.min(n, i + 65536);
      for (; i < e; i++) dst[cnt[(keys[src[i]] >> s) & 0xFFFF]++] = src[i];
      if (i == n) { final t = src; src = dst; dst = t; cnt.fillRange(0, 65537, 0); pass++; stage = 0; i = 0; }
    }
  }
}
```

The result is in `src`. Run it as a phase with the count `() => _rx!.done ? _unit : _unit + 1`.

(2) Split `_sortSweep` into two phases:
- First, a chunked collection of the `_tp != 0` ids into Int32List(_nT), in 65,536-triangle units. Its last unit sets `_rx = _Radix(Int32List.sublistView(buf, 0, m), _tk)` and `_keep = Uint8List(_nT)`.
- Then the radix phase. `_cullChunk` takes `_sweep = _rx!.src` once the radix is done.

(3) Split `_finish` the same way:
- The k1/k2 key loop over _nT, in chunks.
- The two radix sorts (k2, then k1) as _Radix phases.
- The remap fill and mark, and the nv numbering over _nV, in chunks (keep nv as a field so the order is unchanged).
- One unit that allocates the output arrays.
- The compaction over _nV, in chunks.
- The tri/sector fill over `asc`, in chunks.
- One unit that constructs the LanderMesh.

Also cut the sky and cull chunks from 4096 to 2048 triangles. That is safe because both loops run in sequence and keep no state per chunk.

(4) In widgets/lander_view.dart:232, call `runSliced(..., rows: 1, sliceMs: 4)`. A slice is then at most about 4 ms plus one unit of about 4 ms or less, which is within the 8 ms rule.

(5) Optional, native only: build with `Isolate.run(() => buildLanderMesh(...))` through the existing dem_load_io/web conditional-import split, so Android does no mesh work on the UI thread at all. Keep the sliced path for web.

(6) Add a test on the shipped DEM that asserts the largest single step(1) time stays under the budget.

Note: chunking fixes the jank but not the roughly 2.3 s total, which is 10× the 250 ms native budget. Most of that cost comes from the 750 × 750 far patch (about 2.2M triangles, about 97% of them culled). Fixing the total needs either the isolate on native or a smaller or decimated far patch.
